      ":question/id": "f5d4f105-60ef-5ae8-b137-b008bee24cf2",
      ":question/problem": "$\\displaystyle\\int \\left(3-x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(3-x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\left(3-x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &= 3\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &= 3\\frac{x^{0+1}}{(0+1)}-\\frac{x^{2+1}}{(2+1)}+C \\\\ &= 3x-\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
